$ Basis set library file automatically generatered by basis_set.py
$ 
$ invocation: basis_set.py --file=../basis/reference_edited/4p_tz_archive --print_polarization --print_scfbas --uncontracted --equal_exponent_threshold=0.05 --polarization_type=valence correlating,core correlating,diffuse --output=dyall.4p.tz.bas
$ 
$ These are the sets of functions selected from the TCA archive (apart from SCF):
$  ['valence correlating', 'core correlating', 'diffuse']
$ 
$ dyall.4p.tz.bas
$
$ el contained in the file 
$ Ga As Kr Ge Br Se
$ 
$ 4p-Block Triple-Zeta Basis Set Archive
$ ======================================
$ 
$ 
$ Date: 18 Nov 2009
$ 
$ 
$ When publishing work that uses these basis sets, please use the following citation:
$ 
$ K.G. Dyall, Theor. Chem. Acc. (2002) 108:335; erratum Theor. Chem. Acc. (2003) 109:284;
$ revision Theor. Chem. Acc. (2006) 115:441. Basis sets available from the Dirac web site, 
$ http://dirac.chem.sdu.dk.
$ 
$ 
$ 
$ This archive contains the exponents and the SCF spinor or orbital coefficients, 
$ given with respect to normalized (spherical or 2-spinor) Gaussian functions, for
$ the double-zeta basis sets described in the above articles.  
$ 
$ NOTE: Functions are counted from the function with the smallest exponent to the largest. 
$ 
$ The SCF exponent sets were generated as follows:
$ 
$ * Optimization of the s, p, and d sets separately, on the total energy of the
$   configuration average of the s^2 p^n configuration, to yield a 23s 16p 9d
$   set.
$ * Replacement of the outermost SCF d exponent with the valence correlating 2d
$   exponent set and reoptimization of the other 8 d exponents, with the
$   correlating d exponents fixed
$ 
$ The resulting primitive basis set is a 23s 16p 10d set. In all the following
$ descriptions, this is the set referred to as the SCF exponent set. 
$ 
$ The correlating exponents were generated as follows
$ 
$ * A 2d1f exponent set for 4s4p correlation was optimized in MR-SDCI calculations
$   on the ground configuration, with 2 s and 2 p exponents from the SCF set
$   included in the correlating space and held fixed. (These are the 1st and 3rd
$   s and p exponents, determined to be the optimal choice for valence correlation).
$ * A 2f1g exponent for 3d correlation was optimized in MR-SDCI calculations on
$   the ground configuration, with double excitations from the 3d shell into the f
$   shell coupled to J=0.
$ 
$ The diffuse exponent sets were generated as follows.
$ 
$ * An s exponent and a p exponent were optimized in SCF calculations on the
$   negative ion for Ga - Br, with the remaining s and p exponents fixed
$ * A d and an f exponent were optimized in MR-SDCI calculations on the negative 
$   ion for Ga - Br, with 2 s and 2 p exponents from the SCF set, the correlating
$   2d1f set, and the diffuse sp set included in the correlating space, i.e. a 
$   3s3p3d2f correlating space.
$ * The s, p, d, and f exponents were extrapolated in a geometric sequence from Se
$   and Br to obtain exponents for Kr.
$ 
$ 
$ The archive is set out as follows.
$ 
$ 1. Exponents and SCF coefficients for Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave functions, in order
$    of Z. The large component functions are given first, followed by the small 
$    component functions. Both components are labelled with the large component
$    symmetry type, but the primitive functions for the small component are
$    those obtained by restricted kinetic balance from the large component.
$    The spin-orbit components have a "-" label for j=l-1/2 and a "+" label for
$    j=l+1/2.
$ 
$ 2. Exponents and SCF coefficients for spin-free modified Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave
$    functions, in order of Z. The large component functions are given first, 
$    followed by the pseudo-large component functions, then the Foldy-Wouthuysen
$    transformed large component functions. Note that all components have the same
$    symmetry type. 
$ 
$ 3. Exponents of diffuse s, p, d and f functions.
$ 
$ 4. Exponents of valence correlating functions.
$ 
$ 5. Exponents of core correlating functions.
$ 
$ 
$ Basis set construction.
$ ----------------------
$ 
$ Several different types of basis sets may be constructed by adding primitive 
$ functions. These basis sets are described below.
$ 
$ NOTE: Functions are counted from the function with the smallest exponent to the largest. 
$ 
$ 1. Valence (equivalent to cc-pvtz). To the SCF occupied set, add the following:
$    1st and 3rd s primitives from the SCF set
$    1st and 3rd p primitives from the SCF set
$    1st and 2nd d primitives from the SCF set (correlating 2d)
$    valence correlating 1f function.
$ 
$    (HF + 2s2p2d1f valence)
$ 
$ 2. Core-valence. To basis set 1, add the following:
$    4th and 6th s primitives from the SCF set
$    4th and 6th p primitives from the SCF set
$    3rd and 4th d primitives from the SCF set
$    core correlating 2f1g functions.
$ 
$    (HF + 2s2p2d1f valence + 2s2p2d2f1g core)
$ 
$ 3. Dipole polarization of the (n-1)d shell. To basis set 2, add the geometric mean
$    of the valence correlating f exponent and the smallest core correlating f exponent.
$ 
$    The optimal f exponent is not in the correlating sets.
$ 
$ 4. Diffuse basis: add the diffuse s, p, d and f functions to any of the above
$    basis sets.
$ 
$ 
$ 
$ 
$ 

