$ 
$ Basis set library file automatically generatered by basis_set.py
$ 
$ invocation: basis_set.py --file=../basis/reference_edited/7p_tz_archive --print_polarization --print_scfbas --uncontracted --equal_exponent_threshold=0.05 --polarization_type=valence 7s7p correlating,outer core 6d correlating,diffuse --output=dyall.7p.tz.bas
$ 
$ These are the sets of functions selected from the TCA archive (apart from SCF):
$  ['valence 7s7p correlating', 'outer core 6d correlating', 'diffuse']
$ 
$ dyall.7p.tz.bas
$
$ el contained in the file 
$ Uut Uuq Uup Uus Uuo Uuh
$ 
$ 7p Block Triple-Zeta Basis Set Archive
$ ======================================
$ 
$ 
$ Date: 5 Aug 2009
$ 
$ 
$ When publishing work that uses these basis sets, please use the following citation:
$ 
$ K.G. Dyall, in preparation. Basis sets are available from the Dirac web site, 
$ http://dirac.chem.sdu.dk.
$ 
$ 
$ 
$ This archive contains the exponents and the SCF spinor or orbital coefficients,
$ given with respect to normalized (spherical or 2-spinor) Gaussian functions, for
$ the basis sets described in the above articles. The SCF exponent sets reported
$ include the valence correlating d functions. The d sets were reoptimized after
$ replacement of the outer two SCF d functions with the valence correlating d 
$ functions, which were held fixed. The outermost two exponents of the f set were 
$ replaced by the core 6d correlating f exponents, and the remaining f exponents 
$ were reoptimized. In all the following descriptions, this is the set referred to
$ as the SCF exponent set. The diffuse functions were optimized in correlated 
$ calculations on the negative ion, except for Uuo, where they were optimized for 
$ dipole polarization of the 7p shell. 
$ 
$ 
$ 
$ This archive contains the exponents and the SCF spinor or orbital coefficients, 
$ given with respect to normalized (spherical or 2-spinor) Gaussian functions, for
$ the double-zeta basis sets described in the above articles.  
$ 
$ NOTE: Functions are counted from the function with the smallest exponent to the largest. 
$ 
$ The SCF exponent sets were generated as follows:
$ 
$ * Optimization of the s, p, d, and f sets separately, on the total energy of the
$   configuration average of the s^2 p^n configuration, to yield a 30s 29p 20d 12f
$   set.
$ * Replacement of the outermost 2 SCF d exponents with the valence correlating 2d
$   exponent set and reoptimization of the other 18 d exponents, with the
$   correlating d exponents fixed
$ * Replacement of the outermost 2 SCF f exponent with the 5d correlating 2f
$   exponent set, and reoptimization of the other 10 f exponents with the
$   correlating f exponents fixed.
$ 
$ The resulting primitive basis set is a 30s 29p 20d 12f set. In all the following
$ descriptions, this is the set referred to as the SCF exponent set. 
$ 
$ The correlating exponents were generated as follows
$ 
$ * A 2d1f exponent set for 7s7p correlation was optimized in MR-SDCI calculations
$   on the ground configuration, with 2 s and 2 p exponents from the SCF set
$   included in the correlating space and held fixed. (These are the 1st and 3rd
$   s and p exponents, determined to be the optimal choice for valence correlation).
$ * A 2f1g exponent for 5d correlation was optimized in MR-SDCI calculations on
$   the ground configuration, with double excitations from the 5d shell into the f
$   shell coupled to J=0.
$ 
$ The diffuse exponent sets were generated as follows.
$ 
$ * An s exponent and a p exponent were optimized in SCF calculations on the
$   negative ion for Uut - Uus, with the remaining s and p exponents fixed
$ * A d and an f exponent were optimized in MR-SDCI calculations on the negative 
$   ion for Uut - Uus, with 2 s and 2 p exponents from the SCF set, the correlating
$   2d1f set, and the diffuse sp set included in the correlating space, i.e. a 
$   3s3p3d2f correlating space.
$ * The s, p, d, and f exponents were extrapolated in a geometric sequence from Uuh
$   and Uus to obtain exponents for Uuo.
$ 
$  
$ The archive is set out as follows.
$ 
$ 1. Exponents and SCF coefficients for Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave functions, in
$    order of Z. The large component functions are given first, followed by the
$    small component functions. Both components are labelled with the large
$    component symmetry type, but the primitive functions for the small component
$    are those obtained by restricted kinetic balance from the large component.  
$    The spin-orbit components are labeled with "-" for j=l-1/2 and "+" for j=l+1/2.   
$ 
$ 2. Exponents and SCF coefficients for spin-free modified Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave
$    functions, in order of Z. The large component functions are given first, 
$    followed by the pseudo-large component functions, then the Foldy-Wouthuysen
$    transformed large component functions. Note that all components have the same
$    symmetry type. 
$ 
$ 3. Exponents of diffuse s, p, d, and f functions.
$ 
$ 4. Exponents of valence and outer core correlating functions.
$ 
$ 
$ 
$ Basis set construction.
$ ----------------------
$ 
$ Several different types of basis sets may be constructed by adding primitive
$ functions. These basis sets are described below.
$ 
$ NOTE: Functions are counted from the function with the smallest exponent to the largest. 
$ 
$ 1. Valence flexibility. To the SCF functions, add the following:
$ 
$    1st and 3rd s primitives from the SCF set 
$    1st and 2nd p primitives from the SCF set
$    1st and 2nd d primitives from the SCF set (correlating 2d)
$    valence correlating f
$ 
$ 2. Valence + outer core. This set adds correlation of the n=6 shell, but primarily the
$    6d shell. To set 1, add the following:
$ 
$    5th and 6th s primitives from the SCF set 
$    5th and 6th p primitives from the SCF set 
$    2nd and 3rd d primitives from the SCF set 
$    1st and 2nd f primitives from the SCF set (6d correlating f primitives)
$    outer core correlating g
$ 
$ 3. Diffuse basis: add the diffuse s, p, d, and f functions to any of the above
$    basis sets.
$ 
$ 
$ 
$ 
$ 

